//! [depth-limit]
\section1 Depth limit

A message can contain other messages, and those can contain further
messages, so the nesting of a message can be arbitrarily deep. Parsing
deeply nested input uses a lot of stack, so a malicious or corrupt input
could crash the application. To prevent this, the serializer limits
the nesting depth of messages when it deserializes. By default, the limit
is 100 levels, the same as the default in other protobuf implementations
such as C++ and Java. When the limit is exceeded, deserialize() fails and
lastError() returns QAbstractProtobufSerializer::Error::InvalidFormat.

You can change the limit with the \c{QT_PROTOBUF_NESTING_LIMIT}
environment variable. The value must be an integer of at least 1. The
limit only affects deserialize(), and does not affect serialize().

The environment variable is read once per process, the first time the
limit is needed. If you change it later, the new value is not used. A
value that is not an integer, or is smaller than 1, is ignored, and the
default limit is used.

The top-level message is not counted: with a limit of N, a message can
contain N levels of nested messages. Each map field entry whose value is a
message counts as two levels: the entry and its value.
//! [depth-limit]
